%[text] # Calculate a Loudspeaker from FEA Results
%[text] This example calculates a loudspeaker system with a B&C 21SW152-8 woofer in an enclosure that is described by the results of an FEA model in Ansys: a closed box at the rear of the diaphragm and a horn at the front. See the class pages of [`comp.FeaEnclosure`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','feaenclosure','feaenclosuredoc.m'))) and [`result`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','resultdoc.m'))) for the model.
%%
%[text] ## Create the loudspeaker system
%[text] Create the `lspsys` object `sys`.
sys = lspsys;
%%
%[text] ## Add the FEA enclosure
%[text] Give the system a `comp.FeaEnclosure` with the three result files of Ansys: the pressure averaged over the front and over the rear of the diaphragm, and the pressure at the microphone. The files are on the MATLAB path, so their names are enough. The FEA model imposed a diaphragm velocity of 1 mm/s.
sys.Enclosure = comp.FeaEnclosure;
sys.Enclosure.PressureFrontFileName = "frontloadedhorn_pfront.txt";
sys.Enclosure.PressureRearFileName = "frontloadedhorn_prear.txt";
sys.Enclosure.PressureFarFieldFileName = "frontloadedhorn_prmic.txt";
sys.Enclosure.DiaphragmVelocity = 1e-3;
%%
%[text] ## Add the driver
%[text] Mount a `comp.Driver` in the enclosure and give it the Thiele-Small parameters and the optional limits from the datasheet (only needed for the maximum sound pressure level) of the B&C 21SW152-8: the impedances, the nominal power and the excursion $ X\_{var} $.
sys.Enclosure.Driver = comp.Driver;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Re = 6;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Le = 2.2e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qes = 0.38;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qms = 6.4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Fs = 32;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Sd = 1680e-4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Vas = 200e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Znom = 8;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Zmin = 7;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Pnom = 2000;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Xvar = 16e-3;
%%
%[text] ## Solve the system
%[text] The FEA results cover a limited frequency range, so calculate the system at the frequencies of the result files. Then calculate the result `res`.
sys.Frequency = sys.Enclosure.Frequency;
res = sys.createResult;
%%
%[text] ## Loads on the diaphragm
%[text] The front and the rear pressure of the FEA model give the loads on the diaphragm, which `zaFront` and `zaRear` return: $ Z\_{a,f} $ of the horn and its radiation at the front, and $ Z\_{a,r} $ of the closed box at the rear. At low frequency the rear load falls with frequency, like the compliance of a closed volume; the front load shows the resonances of the horn.
f = sys.Frequency;
Zf = sys.Enclosure.zaFront(f,sys.RadiationAngle);
Zr = sys.Enclosure.zaRear(f,sys.RadiationAngle);
loglog(f,abs(Zf),f,abs(Zr),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:50ec8316]
grid on %[output:50ec8316]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:50ec8316]
ylabel("Acoustic impedance [Pa s/m^3]") %[output:50ec8316]
legend("Front: horn, Z_{a,f}","Rear: closed box, Z_{a,r}",Location="northeast") %[output:50ec8316]
title("Loads on the diaphragm from the FEA model") %[output:50ec8316]
%%
%[text] ## Electrical impedance
%[text] The electrical impedance of the loudspeaker system, with the loads of the FEA model on the diaphragm.
semilogx(res.Frequency,abs(res.ElectricalImpedance),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:8f3b67a6]
grid on %[output:8f3b67a6]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:8f3b67a6]
ylabel("Electrical impedance [\Omega]") %[output:8f3b67a6]
title("Electrical impedance") %[output:8f3b67a6]
%%
%[text] ## Sound pressure level at the microphone
%[text] The pressure at the microphone is the transfer $ H\_{mic} $ of the FEA model times the diaphragm volume velocity of the system, so it holds the horn, the diffraction and the directivity of the FEA model. The microphone is at the position of the FEA model.
semilogx(res.Frequency,res.SoundPressureLevel,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:23fffa5e]
grid on %[output:23fffa5e]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:23fffa5e]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:23fffa5e]
title("Sound pressure level at the microphone") %[output:23fffa5e]
%%
%[text] ## Maximum sound pressure level at the microphone
%[text] The highest level at the microphone that the limits allow, for a sine signal. The excursion limit is `Xvar` of the driver, as its `ExcursionLimit` chooses. The power limit of the result is set to a number, `res.PowerLimit = 4000`: 4000 W of real power into the actual electrical impedance of the system, instead of `Pnom` at the minimum impedance of the datasheet. Around the resonances, where the impedance is high, this allows more voltage. The grey lines are the levels that the power (dark) and the excursion (light) allow alone; the lower of both sets the maximum, the dashed blue line.
res.PowerLimit = 4000;
splMax = res.splMax;
powerLevel = res.splPowerLimited;
excursionLevel = res.splExcursionLimited;
colors = colororder;
semilogx(res.Frequency,powerLevel,Color=[0.45 0.45 0.45],LineWidth=1,DisplayName="Power limit") %[output:59f0b264]
hold on %[output:59f0b264]
semilogx(res.Frequency,excursionLevel,Color=[0.75 0.75 0.75],LineWidth=1,DisplayName="Excursion limit") %[output:59f0b264]
semilogx(res.Frequency,splMax,"--",Color=colors(2,:),LineWidth=1.5,DisplayName="Maximum") %[output:59f0b264]
hold off %[output:59f0b264]
grid on %[output:59f0b264]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:59f0b264]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:59f0b264]
xlim([10 400]) %[output:59f0b264]
ylim([100 170]) %[output:59f0b264]
legend(Location="northwest") %[output:59f0b264]
title("Maximum sound pressure level at the microphone") %[output:59f0b264]

